slang: --allow-virtual-iface-hier-ref

"interface containing hierarchical reference to object outside its body cannot be used in a
virtual interface" (VirtualIfaceHierRef) for an interface with an upward reference (e.g. a
probe interface bound into the DUT) that a testbench assigns to a virtual interface.
IEEE 1800-2023 25.9 forbids it; commercial simulators accept it. The new
CompilationFlags::AllowVirtualIfaceHierRef (driver option --allow-virtual-iface-hier-ref)
skips that check in Compilation::checkVirtualIfaceInstance; the interface-port check
(VirtualIfaceIfacePort) is kept. Applies on top of 0009 (same enum).

Under the flag, the instance InstanceSymbol::createVirtual builds for the virtual interface
type is also uninstantiated. That copy is parented to the declaring scope, not to the
instance it will refer to, so a name relative to a bind target (u_core.x in an interface
bound into the module holding u_core) cannot resolve there: slang reported "use of
undeclared identifier" for a name that resolves in every real instance. slang already drops
those reports in uninstantiated bodies; the real instances still check every name. A set
covergroup bins check (CoverageSetType) no longer reports against a coverpoint whose type is
an error, which such a name gives in the copy.

--- a/include/slang/ast/Compilation.h
+++ b/include/slang/ast/Compilation.h
@@ -147,9 +147,14 @@
